{"name":"Monument Valley","hex":"#ad5c34","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Monument Valley","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A region of the Colorado Plateau on the Arizona–Utah state line, famous for its vast sandstone buttes."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Monument_Valley"}],"entries":[{"title":"Monument Valley","blocks":[{"text":"Monument Valley is a region of the Colorado Plateau characterized by a cluster of sandstone buttes, with the largest reaching 1,000 ft (300 m) above the valley floor. The most famous butte formations are located in northeastern Arizona along the Utah–Arizona state line. The valley is considered sacred by the Navajo Nation, the Native American people within whose reservation it lies."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Monument_Valley%2C_Utah%2C_USA_%2823611451292%29.jpg/330px-Monument_Valley%2C_Utah%2C_USA_%2823611451292%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument_Valley","wikidata":"Q192017"},{"title":"Monument Valley (video game)","blocks":[{"text":"Monument Valley is a 2014 puzzle and indie game by Ustwo Games. The player leads the princess Ida through mazes of optical illusions and impossible objects while manipulating the world around her to reach various platforms. Monument Valley was developed over ten months beginning in early 2013 based on concept drawings by company artist Ken Wong. Its visual style was inspired by Japanese prints, minimalist sculpture, and indie games Windosill, Fez, and Sword & Sworcery, and was compared by critics to M. C. Escher drawings and Echochrome."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/1/1a/Monument_Valley_icon_unrounded.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument_Valley_(video_game)","wikidata":"Q15553298"},{"title":"Monument","blocks":[{"text":"A monument is a type of structure created to commemorate a person or event, or which has become relevant to a social group as a part of their remembrance of historic times, or cultural heritage, due to its artistic, historical, political, technical or architectural importance. Examples of monuments include statues, (war) memorials, historical buildings, and cultural assets. If there is a public interest in its preservation, a monument can for example be listed as a UNESCO World Heritage Site."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Monumento_ao_Cristo_Redentor.jpg/330px-Monumento_ao_Cristo_Redentor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Monument","wikidata":"Q4989906"},{"title":"Valley","blocks":[{"text":"A valley is an elongated low area often running between hills or mountains and typically containing a river or stream running from one end to the other. Most valleys are formed by erosion of the land surface by rivers or streams over a very long period. Some valleys are formed through erosion by glacial ice. These glaciers may remain present in valleys in high mountains or polar areas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG/330px-Salta-VallesCalchaquies-P3140151.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Valley","wikidata":"Q39816"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg/500px-Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg/1280px-Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/60/Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg/500px-Forrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"View of Monument Valley in Utah, looking south on U.S. Route 163 from 13 miles (21 km) north of the Utah–Arizona state line","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AForrest_Gump_Point_Monument_Valley_November_2018_001.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG/500px-The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG/1280px-The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG/500px-The_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mitchell Mesa from the View Hotel.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AThe_View_Hotel_in_the_Monument_Valley_Arizona.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg/500px-Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg/1280px-Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg/500px-Monument_Valley%2C_Apache_scout.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=pa